> You can change the color of the jokes to red using CSS in a few different ways, depending on how specific you want to be and how you want to structure your code. Here are a few options:

**Option 1: Targetting the `#joke-container` directly**

This is the simplest approach and generally the best if you *only* want to change the color of the jokes themselves.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Random Joke Generator</title>
<style>
  body {
    font-family: sans-serif;
    text-align: center;
  }
  #joke-container {
    margin-top: 20px;
    font-size: 1.2em;
    color: red; /* Add this line */
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>

<button onclick="generateJoke()">Get a Joke</button>

<div id="joke-container">
  <!-- Joke will appear here -->
</div>

<script>
  const jokes = [
    "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
    "Parallel lines have so much in common. It’s a shame they’ll never meet.",
    "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
    "What do you call a fish with no eyes? Fsh!"
  ];

  function generateJoke() {
    const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
    const joke = jokes[randomIndex];
    document.getElementById("joke-container").innerText = joke;
  }
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

Just add `color: red;` to the CSS rule for the `#joke-container` element.  This will make all the text that appears inside the `#joke-container` red.

**Option 2: Using a CSS Class**

This approach is more flexible if you might want to apply the red color to other elements later, or if you want to change the color back easily.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Random Joke Generator</title>
<style>
  body {
    font-family: sans-serif;
    text-align: center;
  }
  #joke-container {
    margin-top: 20px;
    font-size: 1.2em;
  }
  .red-joke {  /* Define a class */
    color: red;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>

<button onclick="generateJoke()">Get a Joke</button>

<div id="joke-container">
  <!-- Joke will appear here -->
</div>

<script>
  const jokes = [
    "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
    "Parallel lines have so much in common. It’s a shame they’ll never meet.",
    "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
    "What do you call a fish with no eyes? Fsh!"
  ];

  function generateJoke() {
    const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
    const joke = jokes[randomIndex];
    document.getElementById("joke-container").innerText = joke;
    document.getElementById("joke-container").classList.add("red-joke"); // Add the class
  }
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

1.  **Define the CSS Class:**  Create a CSS class named `red-joke` that sets the `color` property to `red`.
2.  **Add the Class in JavaScript:**  Inside the `generateJoke()` function, *after* setting the joke text, add the line `document.getElementById("joke-container").classList.add("red-joke");`.  This adds the `red-joke` class to the `#joke-container` element.

**Option 3: Inline Style (Not Recommended)**

While possible, using inline styles is generally discouraged because it makes your code less maintainable.

```html
<div id="joke-container" style="color: red;">
  <!-- Joke will appear here -->
</div>
```

**Which option to choose?**

* **Option 1 (Direct Styling):**  Best for simple, one-off styling.  Easy to understand and implement.
* **Option 2 (CSS Class):**  More flexible and maintainable, especially if you think you might want to reuse the styling or change it later.  It separates the styling from the JavaScript logic.
* **Option 3 (Inline Style):** Avoid this unless you have a very specific reason to use it.

I recommend Option 1 for this simple example because it's the most straightforward.  However, if you're learning CSS and want to practice using classes, Option 2 is a good choice.
